So there are plenty of architectures, but I prefer API Gateways, message queues, cache, and circular database replication schemes for the database redundancy, integrity management, and duplication benefits

I picked API Gateways over load balancers because of the wide variety of services you’ve requested (ie. not just sending e-mails)

Multiple message queues should also be used, to be able to handle 1000 notifications / second.  MQs have the added benefit of adding a layer of redundancy in case AWS, databases, etc. go down for a few seconds, minutes, or hours.  You especially don’t want to lose important customer data like payment information, passwords, etc. so it’s important to have redundant copies of all this critical data

Finally, circular replication provides more redundancy by copying every database entry multiple times in multiple physical and virtual  locations, and improves latency whenever data is not in cache

These proposals balance availability and consistency, making sure the customer gets their notifications promptly while also waiting for database replication to back up all the data in the silent backend

The cache layer is also critical for the availability side of the equation, though depending on our available budget, we may want to buy less cache and rely more on database reads.






