     	Results:  I turned the team away from a priority of Decisioning 2.0 that would have wasted months and months of productivity on a new engine that didn't have measurable improvements over the previous, existing, tested, decisioning orchestrator / engine that we currently use and thrive on.                                  
     	Situation:  I'm sitting in a meeting room with my Manager, my skip level manager, and my Product Owner, deciding how to prioritize the upcoming quarter.  My Product Owner is really gung ho about Decisioning 2.0, which I personally have thought is a terrible, terrible priority for months and months, ever since I heard about the initiative.  Basically, D2.0 just means rewriting all our Credit Card decisioning code because "there's too much of it" or "no one who wrote the original code is still on the team" or "it's just too much to maintain".  Well let me tell you, you know what makes building on and fixing a complicatedly large amount of code even more complicated?  More code!  Duplicate code!  Writing a second version of the same code to have to pass all the thousands of same unit, integration tests that our team has already written for the decade before us, that works, that is secure for our customers,  and is backwards compatible in case a customer applies for a credit card again for the first time in 5 years.                                    
     	Action:   So what was the action in the STAR format of this story?   I spoke up.  My Product Owner made a face at me, and I was uncomfortable in that meeting, but I did speak up and committed to my opinion, which I still hold as valuable to the larger organization, our shareholders, and our customers.  If we're going to devote our millions of dollars, hundreds of man hours of productivity, stress, etc. to writing new code, it had better have a good purpose.  
     	Result(s):    So what was the result in the STAR format of this story?  I saved the team hundreds of hours of work on rewriting working, tested, documented, extensible code.  This saved the team money, fitting Amazon's Leadership Principle of Frugality, saved the team and adjacent teams time and money by Thinking Big, and also demonstrated Amazon's principle "Are Right, A LOT" principle, too.  I was right.  I had been paying attention in meetings, asking questions, doing my job, reflecting on what I'd heard, and came to the correct conclusion that the whole project was basically a waste of time and (in this case) should be cut entirely  .  If there are specific parts of the code that we have a reason to criticize, rewrite, document,  , that's one thing.  But when we start talking about , the infamous "Mythical Man Month" from the software industry starts kicking in and we should just reject the project entirely, or at the very worst put it way way back in the backlog to be gotten to in 2 years, give or take.                                                                    
    	The principle demonstrated above      was "Disagree And Commit."                                  
  Insist on the Highest Standards:             I insisted we have no stories related to D2.0 in the next 3 months                                         
